WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.041
Hello! Welcome to another video on

00:00:04.416 --> 00:00:05.291
Bug Bounty Reports Explained channel! 

00:00:05.291 --> 00:00:08.333
This time it's a new series called Bounty Vlog.

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:09.833
What is it about? 

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:15.708
Well, something I don't like about social media
 is that it can make all of us feel worthless.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.416
Chances are that if you open Twitter feed right now,
 you will see tweets like "I earned

00:00:21.416 --> 00:00:26.833
X amount of dollars
 today" or "I reported 3 criticas in 2 hours" or something like that.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:32.833
I do this sometimes myself,
 but I generally don't like it, and I know it doesn't help anyone.

00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:40.041
On the other hand, I see people like Shubs
 who show us all their journey, and I hugely, hugely respect it.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:47.791
And I would like to do something similar or maybe even better, because
 I would like to show you my journey from the beginning - from right now.

00:00:48.875 --> 00:00:50.208
How much time I spend? 

00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:51.958
How many vulnerabilities I find? 

00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:54.958
How different methodologies and programs work for me?

00:00:55.375 --> 00:01:02.333
I think this will be especially valuable a few years from now
 after I gain a lot of experience and I become successful.

00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:08.333
Today is the 0th episode where I will show you what my background
 was, my I.T.

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:15.208
security journey so far and how I was able to leave my pentesting job
 with such a small YouTube channel.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:16.791
Enjoy! 

00:01:16.791 --> 00:01:18.000
I started my I.T. 

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.041
security journey quite late - just before going to university.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.958
First, I found out about CTFs and they were absolutely awesome for me.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:35.541
Then, I found out there is a profession called pentester, and back then
 I thought it just like CTFs, except you're getting paid for it.

00:01:35.958 --> 00:01:40.000
But back then, I didn't know how hard it is to get to the field.

00:01:40.208 --> 00:01:46.166
I thought that I would need a few years of university,
 then a few years of programing experience,

00:01:46.291 --> 00:01:50.708
and only then I could aspire to be a pentester. But I was wrong.

00:01:50.916 --> 00:01:58.541
After a year of university, along with learning security
 in my free time, I got a job as a pentester on my second year.

00:01:59.166 --> 00:02:03.208
It was a dream, but I never felt that I'm good enough to stop learning.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.541
I'm generally a learning junkie. 

00:02:05.541 --> 00:02:14.416
I want to optimize and learn everything from IT security tools, through
 time organization to even such simple things like peeling an orange.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.000
I just want to do it as well as I can.

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.166
And bug bounty seems like a perfect fit for me

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:26.791
because here, unless you are doing a lot of recon,
 you won't find a simple XSS in the commonly used parameter.

00:02:27.041 --> 00:02:30.791
There are a lot of good write ups, and I learn a ton reading them.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.166
And that's how Bug Bounty Reports Explained channel was born.

00:02:35.458 --> 00:02:40.791
Originally,
 I just did it to document my learning journey and have a personal brand

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.916
to maybe start doing it
 more seriously a few years from now after I'm bored doing pentests.

00:02:47.916 --> 00:02:52.250
But fast forward one and a half year, and I'm kinda doing it
 full time now.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.833
I didn't want to continue doing both those things simultaneously
 because they just take too much time.

00:02:59.291 --> 00:03:06.666
Instead, I decided that I am in a position to quit my job,
 even though I was making pretty much no money from YouTube.

00:03:06.916 --> 00:03:08.833
It was just more interesting to me

00:03:08.833 --> 00:03:16.708
and I didn't have a specific plan - I just wanted to see what happens
 when I invest more time into this and into bug bounty.

00:03:16.958 --> 00:03:21.291
Do I make more money now than I did as a pentester? Definitely not.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.750
But when quitting my job,
 I had about a year worth of savings on my account, and right now

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:32.958
I'm in a very comfortable position
 where I don't need to make money because I live off of my savings.

00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:39.083
Everything I make through bounties, BBRE
 Premium or sponsorship deals is a kind of bonus to me.

00:03:39.583 --> 00:03:44.000
Right now, I focus on learning and growing,
 and I hope that money will follow.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.791
And actually, it seems like they do

00:03:46.958 --> 00:03:53.833
because it's been four months and instead of burning my savings,
 I'm actually month-to-month extending them.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:04:01.916
Apart from my bounties, I'd like to thank BBRE Premium subscribers and
 companies like the Detectify who sponsor the content on this channel.

00:04:02.291 --> 00:04:06.958
It's also awesome to be my own boss and I have much more free time now.

00:04:07.416 --> 00:04:14.458
Even if in some time I decide that I want to go back
 to just being employed and getting a stable salary month to month,

00:04:14.791 --> 00:04:20.000
I will be happy to have taken this risk and experience
 what I'm experiencing right now.

00:04:20.958 --> 00:04:22.250
The reason I created the 

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:27.500
channel and the newsletter is the lack of good quality content
 that is not for beginners.

00:04:27.791 --> 00:04:34.666
Even though some marketing gurus think I'm making a mistake
 because beginners are always the biggest audience.

00:04:34.958 --> 00:04:41.833
And I agree that with doing a web security course for beginners,
 I may have better financial results.

00:04:42.083 --> 00:04:47.625
What I am doing right now is just much more interesting to me,
 and this is the most important thing.

00:04:48.041 --> 00:04:52.625
The same goes for BBRE Premium.
 You can learn web security with me there

00:04:52.666 --> 00:05:00.750
if you are not interested in totally beginner content. Every two weeks,
 I send an email with tons of hacking tips and tool tutorials.

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:07.041
I also don't have a Patreon or Buy Me A Coffee,
 so that's the only way to support me right now.

00:05:07.541 --> 00:05:11.458
I am not that good at marketing,
 so there aren't hundreds of people there.

00:05:11.583 --> 00:05:16.250
But the most important thing is that
 those that are there are very happy with it.

00:05:18.208 --> 00:05:20.666
What is the bug bounty in all that you might ask?

00:05:20.958 --> 00:05:24.166
Since day one, I learned about them, It was very appealing to me.

00:05:24.541 --> 00:05:30.125
Working from any location in the world,
 being able to self manage my time and finally,

00:05:30.125 --> 00:05:35.541
being awarded based on the performance and not on time
 put is something that I want to do.

00:05:35.875 --> 00:05:40.833
Especially that I strongly believe
 that if I put the time into this , I will succeed.

00:05:41.333 --> 00:05:43.416
But I made a lot of mistakes. 

00:05:43.416 --> 00:05:47.916
At the beginning,
 I just didn't have the web security skills that are necessary.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:55.500
Then, I was approaching the bug bounties like a pentest because
 I was a pentester and I thought bug bounty is easy, but it wasn't.

00:05:56.583 --> 00:06:01.625
With full time studies and part time work,
 I also didn't have much time to do that.

00:06:01.625 --> 00:06:08.833
And since I created the channel, it got even worse
 and I didn't even want to spend my remaining time on bug bounty.

00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:16.583
So even though I consider myself a great pentester and I have strong web
 security skills, in bug bounty, I'm just a beginner,

00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:18.375
just like many of you. 

00:06:18.375 --> 00:06:24.583
Now I want to spend roughly 50% of my time on content creation
 and the other 50%

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:29.541
on bug bounty,
 although so far I am not very good at maintaining this balance.

00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:34.541
But anyway, I am finding some bugs
 which is an absolutely amazing feeling.

00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:40.666
And in this series,
 I will show you my journey with exact numbers, time spent and so on.

00:06:41.083 --> 00:06:45.833
Since my podcast with David Schutz,
 I am focusing on the public bug bounty program

00:06:45.833 --> 00:06:50.833
on Hackerone
 without any recon - just focusing on understanding the application.

00:06:51.333 --> 00:07:00.000
You can expect the next episode in about a month where I will show you
 what were my results after 100 hours spent on that program.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.666
Right now, I'm about at 60 hours.

00:07:02.666 --> 00:07:10.208
And I won't to tell you, how exactly is it going but I will just tease
 that I found a first bug after only two hours of hunting.

00:07:10.791 --> 00:07:14.500
Make sure subscribe to the channel to not miss that episode.

00:07:14.708 --> 00:07:19.875
And if you are a company that could benefit from advertising to IT
 security audience

00:07:19.875 --> 00:07:25.916
of about 15,000 unique visitors
 per month, feel free to contact me at collab@bugbountyexplained.com

00:07:28.083 --> 00:07:30.583
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye!

